+
+# Decision: Reconciling Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot visit independently. Two workers compare notes after a day in the field, each opens the visit on their own tablet, and each submits a correction — possibly days apart, since a tablet can be offline for 48 hours. The record store receives both, and neither arrival order nor authorship makes one of them more authoritative.
+
+Something has to define what the visit *is* after that. The answer determines the record store's public contract: the submission envelope, what retrieval returns, what review work exists, and which visits are eligible for the partner's Friday CSV. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it, so reversing it later means re-cutting both.
+
+The cost of getting it wrong is scientific rather than operational. A visit holds an observation date, an observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. These fields are not independent: a revised species identification and its cover estimate are one judgment expressed in two places. A mechanism that treats them as separable can produce a stored visit that no observer ever submitted and that no one is prompted to look at.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — every original submission is retained and recoverable** (inherited; the tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit*). An option that resolves concurrency by overwriting fails outright.
+
+**Criteria**
+
+- **Risk of combining scientifically related edits unrecognized** — *major*. The HLD takes *inference of scientific intent* as a non-goal. A record that reads as a single observer's judgment but is actually an automatic composite is the failure mode this criterion grades.
+- **Ongoing maintenance burden of the reconciliation logic** — *major*. From the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Logic that needs revisiting whenever the field schema changes has no owner on this team.
+- **Coordinator review volume before Friday** — *moderate*. Two coordinators handle all review work for roughly 6,000 visits a season.
+- **Completeness of the Friday export** — *moderate*. From the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*: a visit still under review is held out of the CSV, so review volume and unresolved backlog translate directly into rows the science partner does not receive that week. This criterion cuts against the selection below.
+- **Inspectability of the path from submission to exported value** — *moderate*. From the tenet of the same name.
+- **Tablet submission and storage footprint** — *minor*. 12 shared tablets, 48-hour disconnection, visits of a few hundred bytes to a few kilobytes.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it was edited from and carries the complete field set. When the named base is the visit's current revision, the submission simply becomes current. When it is not — because another worker already extended it — both submissions stand as competing complete revisions and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator reads them side by side and submits a resolution: a complete field set of their own, superseding the competing set. The unit of concurrency is the visit as the observer submitted it, never a field.
+
+*Demonstration.* Worker A corrects a misspelled observer name; worker B adds a species row that was missed. Both edited from revision 3. The store holds revisions 4 and 5 as competing, and a coordinator produces revision 6 containing both corrections — having seen that they were in fact unrelated.
+
+- Gate: **passes.** The revision chain is append-only; both originals remain readable with their authors and receipt times.
+- Related-edit risk (major): **strong.** No field values are ever combined by the server; every combination is authored by a coordinator who saw both visits whole.
+- Maintenance burden (major): **strong.** Base-revision comparison and supersession are schema-independent — adding a scientific field changes no reconciliation logic.
+- Review volume (moderate): **weak.** Edits to entirely unrelated fields generate review work identical to genuinely conflicting ones.
+- Export completeness (moderate): **weak.** Every concurrent edit, however trivial, holds its visit out of the CSV until a coordinator clears it.
+- Inspectability (moderate): **strong.** A single chain from first submission through each resolution; the exported value is one named revision.
+- Tablet footprint (minor): **partial.** Each submission sends the whole visit and each draft retains a base revision ID; no base snapshot is needed.
+
+*The trade: no automatic combination, paid for in coordinator time and in rows deferred past Friday.*
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies the base state it started from and the changes it makes. Where two concurrent edits touch disjoint fields, the server combines them and the visit stays exportable with no human involvement; only overlapping changes go to review. Observer lists and species entries merge per entry rather than wholesale, so two workers adding different species both succeed. On this team's edit patterns most concurrency is disjoint, so the common case clears itself and coordinator attention is spent only where there is a real disagreement — the review queue stays small and the Friday export stays full.
+
+- Gate: **passes.** Original submissions are retained alongside the merged representation; the merge is derived, not destructive.
+- Related-edit risk (major): **weak.** Disjointness is computed over field names, and the relationships that matter are semantic: a corrected identification in the species list and its cover estimate are separate fields, so an automatic merge can produce a composite record with no review prompt. Requiring manual approval of every merged record would address this, but by removing the property the option exists for.
+- Maintenance burden (major): **weak.** Merge rules must be written and maintained per field kind — scalars, the observer list, species entries keyed by taxon — and revisited whenever the field set changes.
+- Review volume (moderate): **strong.** Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator.
+- Export completeness (moderate): **strong.** Most concurrent edits resolve themselves and stay eligible for the week's CSV.
+- Inspectability (moderate): **partial.** Explaining an exported value means field-level provenance — which submission each field came from — rather than naming one revision.
+- Tablet footprint (minor): **partial.** Changes are smaller than whole visits, but each draft must retain a base snapshot to compute them.
+
+*The trade: most concurrency disappears without human attention, at the price of rules that decide separability by field name.*
+
+### Last-write-wins
+
+The most recent submission replaces the stored visit; concurrency is not represented.
+
+- Gate: **eliminated.** The earlier submission is unrecoverable. This is the behavior of the spreadsheet imports the project exists to replace.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The two major criteria both favor it, and they are the ones that bear on the project's purpose. On related-edit risk, whole-visit revisions are strong where merging is weak, and the weakness is not a rule-quality problem that better rules would fix: separability by field name is the wrong test for fields that carry one scientific judgment between them, and the error it produces is silent — a composite record that reads as authored and that nothing flags for a coordinator. On maintenance burden, base comparison and supersession are indifferent to the field set, while merge rules are a standing obligation on a team with no systems administrator to carry it. The decision turns on *do not silently discard a submitted edit* for the gate, on *inference of scientific intent* as a non-goal for the first major criterion, and on *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* for the second.
+
+It is chosen against the two moderate criteria it loses on, and that cost is real and ongoing: a corrected observer name concurrent with an added species — edits that are genuinely unrelated — produces an unresolved visit and a row the science partner does not receive until the following Friday. The judgment is that coordinator time spent confirming unrelated edits is recoverable work, while a silently combined record that crosses a scientific relationship may never be noticed at all. Inspectability reinforces the choice: a single revision chain explains an exported value by naming one revision, where merging explains it per field.
+
+Merging stays worth reconsidering, and these are the conditions that would reopen it. If the unresolved backlog becomes the binding constraint on the Friday export — enough visits deferred that the partner's file is materially incomplete — the question changes from *which mechanism* to *how much of the queue is avoidable*. The form worth revisiting then is narrow: automatic combination restricted to a small, explicitly enumerated set of fields with no scientific relationship to the rest (observer-name spelling, notes appended at different offsets), with everything touching species identification or cover estimates still producing competing revisions. That is a different and smaller commitment than general field-aware merge, and it can be added behind the revision model rather than replacing it — a resolution authored by the server for a whitelisted field set. Choosing whole-visit revisions now does not foreclose it; choosing merge now would foreclose the strong related-edit guarantee, which is the harder thing to recover.
+
+**What this obligates downstream.** Both are cascades into their own segments, recorded here because this decision creates them.
+
+- **tablet-sync** retains a base revision ID per local draft (no base snapshot and no change computation), submits the complete field set against it, and distinguishes three submission outcomes to the worker — advanced, accepted-but-now-competing, and rejected. The middle one is the load-bearing case: it is a successful submission that needs coordinator attention, and must not be presented as a failure to retry or as a plain success. Drafts may be started from a competing revision, so the local model cannot assume a visit has one server-side state.
+- **review-export** needs a comparison view over a visit's competing revisions showing each one's author and receipt time, the resolution operation — including its rejection when a worker submits mid-comparison and the coordinator's superseded set is no longer complete — and export eligibility restricted to resolved visits, holding unresolved visits out of the CSV. It explains an exported value by naming a revision, not by field-level provenance. How coordinators correct an already-resolved visit is open and belongs to that node.
